(let [template (load-file "public/chp/template.chp")]
  (clojure.walk/postwalk-replace
    {:template/title "Datom.World: FAQ"
     :template/content
     (list
       [:section.section-light
        [:div.section-inner
         [:h2 "Datom.World FAQ"]
         [:div.faq-grid
          [:details.faq {:open true}
           [:summary "What is Datom.World?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Datom.World is a local-first, stream-native platform. Streams of datoms flow between devices, and apps interpret those streams according to their domain. "
             "The platform ships "
             [:a {:href "/yin.chp"} "Yin"]
             " (a continuation VM), "
             [:a {:href "/yang.chp"} "Yang"]
             " (a compiler), "
             [:a {:href "/dao-space.chp"} "DaoSpace"]
             " (a tuple space of datom streams), "
             [:a {:href "/post-graphics.chp"} "PostGraphics"]
             " (a graphics frame vocabulary and renderer), and "
             [:a {:href "/shibi.chp"} "Shibi"]
             " (an economic primitive)."]
            [:p
             "All apps work the same way: they consume datom streams and interpret them. DaoSpace interprets them as queryable indexes, PostGraphics interprets graphics frame programs as drawn output, Shibi interprets them as economic signals. "
             "The radical move is unifying code, data, and computation into a single primitive (the datom stream), making programs queryable, mobile, and evolvable."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "What is a datom, and what is a tuple?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "A tuple is an element of any dimension/size in the moduli space. A datom is specifically the canonical persistent five-tuple [e a v t m]: entity, attribute, value, transaction ID, metadata ID. It is an immutable fact, not an object. "
             "The t is monotonic within its stream, not globally ordered. The m is always an integer: reserved low IDs are validity markers, and IDs 16 and above reference stream-local metadata entities that carry causal relationships, encryption context, or expansion provenance. This is how causality is established across streams, since t has no meaning outside its originating stream. "
             "When you transact a map, it decomposes into datoms automatically. See "
             [:a {:href "/dao-space.chp"} "DaoSpace"]
             " for how datoms are indexed and queried, and "
             [:a {:href "/blog/power-of-restriction-datom-tuple.blog"} "The Power of Restriction"]
             " for the tuple's structure and design rationale."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "What does \"everything is a stream\" mean?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Data never freezes in silos. Every fact lives in an append-only stream that can be read, transformed, or extended. "
             "Streams are the single I/O abstraction: files, sockets, queues, and databases collapse into one primitive. "
             "The "
             [:a {:href "/dao-stream.chp"} "DaoStream"]
             " protocol is four operations: open!, next (read), put! (write), close!. Backpressure and cursor-based reading are built in."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "What does \"everything is a continuation\" mean?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "A continuation is a first-class bookmark into an execution. In Yin, functions, closures, coroutines, exceptions, and async tasks all reduce to continuation manipulation. "
             "Because continuations are persistent data structures, a VM state can pause and resume locally as a value. Cross-device migration is the design target of the Universal Continuation Format (UCF): a lifted continuation travels through streams and resumes on a different device with full context intact. Portable heterogeneous migration is not yet complete. "
             "See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/continuations-universal-semantic-kernel.blog"} "Continuations as Universal Semantic Kernel"]
             " and "
             [:a {:href "/blog/computation-moves-data-stays.blog"} "Computation Moves, Data Stays"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Who owns the data flowing through these streams?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "You do. Datoms stay under the author's control from creation through sharing. There is no corporate datastore to monetize or subpoena. "
             "You decide which peers can observe a stream, for how long, and with which capabilities (enforced through "
             [:a {:href "/shibi.chp"} "Shibi"]
             " tokens)."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "How is privacy preserved if everything is streaming?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Privacy is the default. Each datom can bundle access rules, key material, expiration, and audit trails. Encryption happens before transmission, so only authorized peers can interpret the facts they receive. "
             "Execution safety: Yin is designed around explicit primitives and stream-mediated effects, providing the basis for constrained execution. Full portable untrusted-code enforcement remains under development."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "What can I build?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Anything that benefits from shared truth across devices: social spaces, marketplaces, automation meshes, IoT telemetry, multiplayer tooling, or AI agents. "
             "Because streams are universal, the same facts can render as dashboards, maps, spreadsheets, or code triggers without duplicating data. "
             "See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/use-cases-datom-world.blog"} "Use Cases for Datom.World"]
             " for concrete scenarios."]]]]]]

       [:section.section-dark
        [:div.section-inner
         [:h2 "For Clojure programmers"]
         [:p.section-lead
          "Answers aimed at working Clojurists evaluating the project."]
         [:div.faq-grid
          [:details.faq {:open true}
           [:summary "Is this a Clojure library, or something that replaces Clojure?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Neither. The project is written in portable Clojure (.cljc), and Clojure is one of the surface syntaxes you can write programs in. "
             "Yang's yang.clojure frontend reads ordinary Clojure s-expressions and lowers them into the Universal AST, which lowers in turn into flat content-addressed code rows. Yin then executes the resulting instruction vector. "
             "Clojure is a source language. Content-addressed code is the canonical form of programs, and datoms name and version it. The JVM (or Node, or Dart) is the host."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "How does this relate to Datomic?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Datom.World inherits the datom idea from Datomic and generalizes it. First, the transaction ID t is stream-scoped, not globally ordered. Cross-stream causality lives in the metadata slot m. "
             "Second, the canonical d5 datom remains canonical for persistent facts and the reference/ledger layer, while code uses other tuple dimensions and is joined to datoms by content address. "
             "Third, while Datomic queries its fixed datom shape, Datom.World allows n-dimensional tuples and keeps their meaning in the interpreter. "
             [:a {:href "/dao-space.chp"} "DaoSpace"]
             " is the exact associative tuple-space point, not the whole family. See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/daospace-moduli-space-of-databases.blog"} "DaoSpace Is a Tuple Space, Not a Moduli Space"]
             " and "
             [:a {:href "/blog/datomworld-moduli-space-of-databases.blog"} "Datom.world: A Moduli Space of Databases"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Is Yin a replacement for the JVM?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "No. Yin is a portable continuation VM written in .cljc. The JVM is one host, Node.js is another, Dart/Flutter is a third, and there are experimental native paths. "
             "The same Yin program runs unchanged across all of them. See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/semantic-bytecode-benchmarks.blog"} "Semantic Bytecode Benchmarks"]
             " for cross-host measurements."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "How does DaoStream compare to core.async?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "core.async is an application-level library for channels and go blocks. DaoStream is a portable substrate with a descriptor-plus-realization model and pluggable transports (ringbuffer, WebSocket, file, and others). "
             "DaoStream streams are addressable, persistent, and cross-process. core.async channels are not. A DaoStream is closer in spirit to a Datomic log segment plus a core.async channel plus a Unix pipe, collapsed into one primitive."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Can I use existing Clojure libraries?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "On the JVM host, anything Clojure can call is reachable from host code. Calling into external code from inside Yin (including other Clojure libraries, or eventually native C and Rust) goes through "
             [:code "dao.stream.apply"]
             ": function application is reified as a request/response stream pair, with the op and evaluated arguments emitted on the request stream and the result returned on the response stream. There is no direct native FFI. Reaching a C or Rust library requires a bridge that reads the request stream and calls the native function, and such a bridge does not exist yet. "
             "Libraries that assume they own threads, global mutable state, or JVM-specific I/O may not port cleanly to the Node or Dart hosts. Treat Yin as a portable core with host-specific edges. See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/beyond-ffi-yin-vm-datom-streaming.blog"} "Beyond FFI"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "How do I run it? What is the REPL?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Use "
             [:code "yin.repl"]
             ", a portable REPL that evaluates Clojure, Python, or PHP source through Yang into Yin. "
             "You can switch VMs at the prompt with "
             [:code "(vm :semantic)"]
             " or "
             [:code "(vm :ast-walker)"]
             ", switch languages with "
             [:code "(lang :clojure)"]
             ", and connect to a remote yin.repl over WebSocket with "
             [:code "(connect \"daostream:ws://host:port\")"]
             ". The REPL is available on the JVM, Node, and Dart."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "What are the build and test commands?"]
           [:div
            [:ul.bulleted
             [:li [:code "clj -M:test"] " runs the Clojure test suite."]
             [:li [:code "clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test && node target/node-tests.js"] " runs the ClojureScript suite."]
             [:li [:code "clj -M:cljd compile"] " compiles the ClojureDart build."]
             [:li [:code "clj -M:kondo --lint <path>"] " lints a file."]
             [:li [:code "npx shadow-cljs watch demo"] " starts the browser demo in watch mode."]]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "What about macros? Clojure macros are source-to-source."]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Macros still work, but because code is stored as flat content-addressed rows rather than a nested form, the expansion protocol for the row substrate is planned rather than shipped. "
             "The planned protocol gives each macro a "
             [:code ":yin/phase-policy"]
             " of "
             [:code ":compile"]
             ", "
             [:code ":runtime"]
             ", or "
             [:code ":both"]
             ", so the same mechanism covers compile-time expansion and first-class runtime rewrite. "
             "Rows are content without t or m, so provenance will live in the ledger: expansion events are datoms referencing the content addresses involved, and you can query back to the original source. See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/yin-vm-macros.blog"} "Yin.vm Macros"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Do I have to write Clojure?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "No. Yang currently ships Clojure, Python, and PHP frontends. All of them lower to the same Universal AST, so a Clojure lambda and a Python lambda become the same kind of AST node. Mixed-language programs share the same execution and the same dataset."]
            [:p
             "An ANTLR integration is planned, which will turn any existing ANTLR grammar into a viable frontend: drop in the grammar, wire the parse tree to Universal AST nodes, and the language targets Yin. The ecosystem of ANTLR grammars (Java, C, C++, SQL, Kotlin, TypeScript, Go, and so on) becomes a long tail of potential Yang frontends. See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/many-syntaxes-one-ast.blog"} "Many Syntaxes, One AST"]
             " and "
             [:a {:href "/blog/yin-vm-ast-chinese-characters.blog"} "Chinese Characters for Programming Languages"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Why would I pick Yin.VM for Flutter/ClojureDart mobile development?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "ClojureDart does not ship a full socket REPL. That is a real loss: interactive development is one of Clojure's best properties, and mobile work is where you need it most. "
             "Embedding Yin.VM into a Flutter app restores it. The app carries a full "
             [:code "yin.repl"]
             " reachable over WebSocket, so you can connect from your laptop and evaluate code directly inside the running Android or iOS process. "
             "Calls into native platform code go through "
             [:code "dao.stream.apply"]
             ", which reifies FFI as a request/response stream pair: you invoke a bridge from the REPL, the bridge calls native, and the result comes back on the response stream. The REPL workflow and the FFI workflow are the same primitive. "
             "For developers shipping cross-platform mobile apps in Clojure, this is the pragmatic reason to adopt Yin.VM."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Why would I pick this over plain Clojure plus Datomic?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "If your program fits comfortably on one JVM, you probably should not. The project earns its keep when the problem is distributed, local-first, mobile, swarmed, or agent-driven, or when you want code and data in the same queryable substrate. "
             "See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/devils-advocate.blog"} "Devil's Advocate"]
             " for the honest rebuttal to this question."]]]]]]

       [:section.section-dark
        [:div.section-inner
         [:h2 "Datoms, streams, storage"]
         [:p.section-lead
          "The data model underneath the platform."]
         [:div.faq-grid
          [:details.faq {:open true}
           [:summary "How are datoms stored and replicated?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Datoms flow through DaoStreams. Interpreters consume streams and materialize what they need. "
             "The index library maintains append-only covered indexes (EAVT, AEVT, AVET, VAET) and merges datoms by transaction and causal ID. "
             "Each node chooses what to materialize. Some store everything, others keep only recent data or specific attributes."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "What is entanglement between nodes?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Entanglement is a lightweight consensus mechanism. When streams entangle, a leader establishes event ordering, imposing a single monotonic sequence across the entangled streams. "
             "Leadership can shift on demand, which delivers consistency without global locks or blockchains. "
             "Transaction IDs remain stream-scoped even when entangled: cross-stream causality is always determined through metadata."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Why stream-scoped t instead of Datomic's global t?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "A globally monotonic t requires a single source of truth. That is fine for one database; it is a coordination bottleneck for a local-first, offline-capable, peer-to-peer fabric. "
             "Stream-scoped t plus causal metadata is the price of doing without a central transactor. See "
             [:a {:href "/dao-space.chp"} "DaoSpace"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Datomic uses [e a v t op] where op is true for assertions and false for retractions. How does Datom.World's [e a v t m] handle retraction?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Polarity lives in the metadata slot itself: "
             [:code "m"]
             " directly carries the validity marker. The reserved id "
             [:code "0"]
             " marks a retraction and "
             [:code "1"]
             " an assertion, mirroring and extending Datomic's "
             [:code "added"]
             " boolean."]
            [:p
             "The transaction log records each committed tx as a "
             [:code "[t added retracted]"]
             " entry: the set of datoms that were added and the set that were retracted at that t. The query fold removes datoms carrying the retract marker, and indexes (EAVT, AEVT, AVET, VAET, MEAVT) present the current view; the log retains the full history for time-travel and audit."]
            [:p
             "The difference from Datomic's fused "
             [:code "op"]
             " is range, not presence: the low reserved m ids are validity markers, while m ids 16 and above reference metadata entities that carry cross-stream causality and provenance. The datom carries the fact; its m says whether it is live; the metadata entity says why."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "What does the metadata field m carry?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "m is always an integer. Reserved low ids are validity markers, not entity references: 0 is retract, 1 assert, 2 derived. Ids 16 and above reference stream-local metadata entities, which can carry causal predecessors, encryption context, capability requirements, macroexpansion provenance, branch identity, or any domain-specific context. "
             "Because a metadata entity is itself stored as datoms, meta can be queried like any other data."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Are DaoStreams like pi-calculus channels?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Partly. The structural invariants match: streams are first-class, they are the sole I/O primitive, and they are mobile (streams can be sent through streams, which is pi-calculus's defining move). "
             "The runtime model differs: pi-calculus channels are ephemeral rendezvous points with no history, while DaoStreams are append-only sequences with cursor-based non-destructive reads and backpressure. "
             "DaoStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs (binary bytes, EDN values, arbitrary payloads), and most commonly carry datoms, at which point the causal metadata in each datom's m field layers ordering semantics on top of the stream. "
             "A DaoStream is closer to a durable, cursor-addressable pi-calculus channel. The project's own framing is \"beyond pi-calculus\": keep the mobility and first-class channel idea, add a persistent substrate. See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/beyond-pi-calculus.blog"} "Beyond Pi-Calculus"]
             " and "
             [:a {:href "/blog/process-calculus-causal-categories-datom-interpreters.blog"} "Process Calculus, Causal Categories, Datom Interpreters"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "How is data serialized on the wire?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Each layer has its own canonical form, and wire encodings are projections of it. Persistent facts are datoms [e a v t m]. AST code is flat, content-addressed per-node rows. Executable code is a positional instruction vector. A paused execution is a UCF continuation value."]
            [:p
             "On the wire, EDN is used for human-readable interchange, and transit-flavored encodings carry values across transports efficiently. The fact stream is authoritative; the byte encoding is a projection."]]]]]]

       [:section.section-dark
        [:div.section-inner
         [:h2 "Yin and Yang"]
         [:p.section-lead
          "The continuation machine and its compilers."]
         [:div.faq-grid
          [:details.faq {:open true}
           [:summary "What is Yin?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Yin is a universal continuation machine. It evaluates programs by executing a decoded positional instruction image, and its execution state is a persistent CESK record (Control, Environment, Store, Kontinuation). "
             "Because state is a value, you can snapshot, serialize, migrate, fork, and resume VMs cheaply. See "
             [:a {:href "/yin.chp"} "Yin"]
             " and "
             [:a {:href "/blog/the-cesk-machine-control-environment-store-continuation.blog"} "The CESK Machine"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "How does Yin differ from the JVM or EVM?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "The JVM hides runtime state behind bytecode. The EVM prices computation but limits introspection. Yin reifies continuations as data. "
             "That single change is the basis for distributed execution, dynamic transformation, and queries over the running program itself; zero-copy cross-device migration is the UCF design target rather than a completed feature."]]]
          
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "If a running program (a continuation) is saved to the database and moved to a completely different machine, do the internal memory IDs clash? Do I have to manually rewrite them ('parallel transport')?"]
           [:div
            [:p "You do not manually rewrite IDs, provided the continuation is exported in the Universal Continuation Format (UCF). A continuation parked inside one VM still contains that VM's local segment IDs and is not portable by itself."]
            [:p "When the VM lifts the parked state into UCF, it replaces every local segment reference—in the current control state, return and effect frames, and closures—with the content address of the segment's canonical instruction vector. Program counters remain relative to that vector. The UCF value also identifies the complete execution contract needed to interpret the code."]
            [:p "On the receiving machine, the resumer checks the contract, verifies the code's address and tuple grammar, and resolves the address from its code index, code carried with the continuation, or dao.jing. If that code is already loaded, the VM reuses its existing local ID. Otherwise, it may mint a fresh local ID that does not conflict with a live segment. An address-to-local-ID alias records that association without allowing an existing ID to be redirected."]
            [:p "Thus local IDs may exist in a VM's memory or local database, but they do not cross the portable UCF boundary as code identity. The UCF lift/lower process performs the required coordinate translation automatically. (Note: This protocol is currently proposed/deferred rather than a completed heterogeneous-migration feature)."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "There are several VMs. Why?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Historically, four backends explored the AST-as-datoms abstraction under different execution strategies: Semantic (interpreted datoms directly), Register (compiled datoms to register bytecode), Stack (compiled to stack bytecode), and AST Walker (walked the AST tree). "
             "That EAVTM/four-VM design is retired; Register and Stack are gone. Today the Semantic VM executes a decoded positional instruction image — it does not query datoms in its hot loop — and the AST Walker remains as a reference interpreter. See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/a-tale-of-four-vm-benchmarks.blog"} "A Tale of Four VM Benchmarks"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "What is the Yang compiler?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Yang lowers source languages into the Universal AST. Today it ships Clojure, Python, and PHP frontends. Each frontend emits the same AST shape, so Yin executes them interchangeably. "
             "A planned ANTLR integration will let any existing ANTLR grammar (Java, C, C++, SQL, Kotlin, TypeScript, Go, and the rest of the ANTLR ecosystem) serve as the front half of a Yang frontend: supply the grammar, map parse-tree nodes to Universal AST nodes, and the language runs on Yin. "
             "See "
             [:a {:href "/yang.chp"} "Yang"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "How does the Universal AST relate to datoms?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "The layering runs AST → flat rows → instruction vector → datoms. The AST lowers into flat, content-addressed per-node rows; the rows assemble into the positional instruction vector the VM executes; and datoms are the reference/ledger layer that names, versions, and publishes code by content address. "
             "Code becomes queryable data: find every function that calls a given API, trace dependencies, rewrite programs with the same tools you query a database. "
             "See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/ast-higher-dimensional-datom-streams.blog"} "AST as Higher-Dimensional Construction of Datom Streams"]
             " and "
             [:a {:href "/blog/ide-edits-ast-not-text.blog"} "When the IDE Edits AST, Not Text"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Why persistent data structures throughout?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Persistence makes snapshot-and-resume cheap. You get lightweight time travel, safe distributed execution, and resilience without stop-the-world pauses. "
             "The same discipline that powers Clojure's collections powers VM state in Yin."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Are agents programs, or continuations?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Agents "
             [:em "are"]
             " continuations. There is no separate agent abstraction. An agent is a serializable execution state that can pause and resume locally with its full context; cross-device migration is the UCF design target. "
             "Mobility, security, and computation merge into a single primitive. See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/agents.blog"} "Agents"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Does Yin interoperate with other systems?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Yes, but not through a conventional FFI. All external function application goes through "
             [:code "dao.stream.apply"]
             ", a request/response stream pair that reifies the call as data. Any system willing to consume the request stream and emit a result on the response stream can be reached: other language runtimes, remote services, or (once a bridge is written) native C and Rust libraries. "
             "EDN and transit serialize values across language and network boundaries. No native FFI bridge ships yet; the stream-apply primitive is the single interop mechanism. See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/beyond-ffi-yin-vm-datom-streaming.blog"} "Beyond FFI: Yin.VM Datom Streaming"]
             "."]]]]]]

       [:section.section-dark
        [:div.section-inner
         [:h2 "Performance and trade-offs"]
         [:p.section-lead
          "The honest numbers."]
         [:div.faq-grid
          [:details.faq {:open true}
           [:summary "What does queryable tuple code cost?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "The published figures — semantic bytecode (unique node IDs and datom triples for every construct) roughly four to eleven times slower to compile than opaque bytecode, and one to seven times slower to execute, depending on host (JVM, Node, Dart) — are historical measurements of the retired EAVTM/four-VM design. "
             "The current Semantic VM executes a decoded positional instruction image and does not query datoms in its hot loop. What the tuple substrate still buys is introspection: a running program is queryable by the same tools that query your database. See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/semantic-bytecode-benchmarks.blog"} "Semantic Bytecode Benchmarks"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Which of the four VMs was fastest?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "Those rankings are historical measurements of the retired EAVTM/four-VM design: Register led, followed by Stack, then Semantic, then AST Walker, and the Semantic VM was about 50 percent faster than the AST Walker after optimization, which was not the original prediction. Register and Stack have since been retired. The surviving Semantic VM executes a decoded positional instruction image; it does not interpret datoms directly. "
             "See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/a-tale-of-four-vm-benchmarks.blog"} "A Tale of Four VM Benchmarks"]
             " and "
             [:a {:href "/blog/ast-datom-streams-bytecode-performance.blog"} "AST Datom Streams and Bytecode Performance"]
             "."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Should I worry about the overhead?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "If your workload is a tight inner loop on a single host, yes, and you probably want a different tool. If your workload is distributed, agent-driven, mobile, or benefits from code-as-data queryability, the overhead pays for capabilities that the faster VM cannot offer at all."]]]]]]

       [:section.section-dark
        [:div.section-inner
         [:h2 "Shibi and governance"]
         [:p.section-lead
          "The economic primitive and capability tokens."]
         [:div.faq-grid
          [:details.faq {:open true}
           [:summary "What is Shibi?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             [:a {:href "/shibi.chp"} "Shibi"]
             " is the capability token system that coordinates access and resources across Datom.World. Tokens grant specific capabilities: stream access, computational metering, agent migration credentials, rate limiting, cross-agent communication. "
             "Shibi implements Elinor Ostrom's principles for governing digital commons, enabling delegation, attenuation, and auditable resource sharing without central authorities."]]]
          [:details.faq
           [:summary "Is Shibi money or a blockchain token?"]
           [:div
            [:p
             "No. Shibi tokens are purpose-specific, capability-based, and non-transferable by default. Each issuer can mint them freely; value comes from what the token authorizes, not from scarcity. "
             "See "
             [:a {:href "/blog/shibi-as-money.blog"} "Shibi as Money"]
             " and "
             [:a {:href "/blog/attention-market-critiques.blog"} "Attention Market Critiques"]
             "."]]]]]]

       [:section.section-light
        [:div.section-inner
         [:h2 "Still curious?"]
         [:p
          "Bring your scenario or data model and we will explore it together. "
          "Intros, architecture reviews, and partner pilots happen weekly."]
         [:div.callout
          [:p [:strong "Continuation mindset"] ": the system never loses context."]
          [:ul.bulleted
           [:li "User perspective: the web remembers how it flows, so experiences remain seamless, local-first, and privacy-centric."]
           [:li "Developer perspective: every execution state is a first-class continuation that unifies concurrency, recovery, and distribution."]]]
         [:div.hero-actions
          [:a.btn {:href "mailto:hello@datom.world"} "Request a preview session"]
          [:a.btn.btn-outline {:href "/datomworld.chp"} "Return to the overview"]]]])}
    template))
